Yes. The interesting business is not “AI videos.” It’s a small entertainment IP factory where the writing is the scarce asset and every other medium is an interchangeable delivery layer.

What we just accidentally found is almost a dual-attention format: one channel occupies the linguistic/story brain, while another gives the eyes something low-friction to chew on. Instead of asking someone to stare at two talking heads for five minutes, you give them the Nicolas-Cage-fever-dream narration while something visually satisfying keeps moving—gameplay, animation, miniature dioramas, absurd AI reenactments, rooftop footage, restoration videos, map animations, whatever. The visual need not carry the plot; it carries attention continuity.

And the timing is interesting. YouTube just announced that Shorts recommendations will further prioritize original material and reduce reach for straight reuploads; its monetization rules also specifically distinguish serialized original stories from repetitive/template-generated “inauthentic” content. AI disclosure itself does not automatically hurt recommendations or monetization. TikTok, meanwhile, says it is testing stronger detection of accounts pumping out AI spam. So the profitable lane is probably not “generate 400 sludge clips.” It’s recognizable original comedy with AI as the production studio. 

I’d build it like this:

* Core IP: recurring absurdist universe, characters, rules, callbacks, Easter eggs. The Cage Limit, Little Guy recursion, the dead-serious normal person surrounded by impossible lore—that structure is much more valuable than any single joke.
* 15–40 sec clips: one setup + one escalation + hard punchline. Maximum discoverability.
* 60–180 sec episodes: enough time for callbacks and character attachment.
* 10–30 min “story radio” cuts: several episodes woven together over satisfying background footage. People can half-watch/half-listen.
* Animated version: same script rendered as intentionally cheap/weird animation. Cheapness can itself become part of the aesthetic instead of pretending to be Pixar.
* Interactive version: audience votes on the next decision—“Does Little Guy open the salt shaker?”—and the winning branch becomes canon.
* Compilation/podcast: audio-only works surprisingly well because the imagery is already ridiculous enough that listeners construct it themselves.
* Merch later: fictional artifacts from the universe rather than generic branding: THE MANAGEMENT tarot card, “Third Option” quarter, Shadow-Force-style dossiers, Moon Cop badge, etc.

One important commercial change: I would migrate away from depending on Nicolas Cage himself. Parody involving a public figure is one thing; building a whole monetized business around synthetic Nicolas Cage likeness/voice creates unnecessary rights-of-publicity, platform, endorsement, and licensing exposure. You could retain the comic invariant while creating an original protagonist: an impossibly sincere washed-up action actor who experiences ordinary reality as mythological espionage. Then you own the guy. You can exaggerate him indefinitely, license him, animate him, make games, and let other creators use the universe.

The content machine then becomes beautifully efficient:

one funny premise → script → narrated Short → animated Short → gameplay-background cut → illustrated carousel → audio episode → audience branch → longer compilation

Same semantic object, multiple translations. And because we were just talking about what survives translation, this becomes an actual empirical test: if a joke survives text, voice, animation, gameplay-overlay, and audience retelling, you’ve probably found one of the structural jokes worth building the franchise around.

There’s also a nastier little attention trick available: occasionally make the background quietly interact with the narration. Ninety percent of the time it’s mindless visual motion. Then the narrator says, “Little Nick noticed something behind him,” and a one-inch character has actually been standing unnoticed in the gameplay footage for twenty seconds. Now viewers realize the supposedly disposable visual channel contains lore too. Rewatch rate goes up because the background suddenly matters.

That transforms “brain-rot background footage” from filler into a second narrative layer.

And that I think has legs.

Because then we’re no longer making disposable clips. We’re building a comedy universe disguised as disposable clips.

I can monitor Shorts/TikTok creator-policy and format shifts and only flag changes that materially affect this model.